e liberal party and  is a combat veteran himself the trudeau  government has promised an independent  probe into the allegations  but over six weeks later any movement on  that promise has  yet to materialize this week on the west  block i'll ask dominic leblanc one of  justin trudeau's most  influential cabinet ministers and the  person in charge of the privy council  office  when he will be able to tell canadians  and our troops  more about how the investigation will  unfold  we have been obviously very concerned  about these allegations  and the prime minister and the defense  minister have said  that we recognize the need to have a  robust uh  review of these allegations uh that's  expeditious but that's fair  uh and we're committed to doing that and  the minister of defense and the prime  minister will have more to say  in in the coming days the government has  also promised to create a military  sexual misconduct watchdog  but there are few details on what that  would look like experts say for it to be  effective it must be insulated from the  chain of command  and that likely means it would have to  report to parliament and not the  minister of national defense  so far the government won't commit to  that or say  quite what the plan is we have said that  changes  will be forthcoming we have said that  and we have shown across the board as a  government  uh that these allegations and these  circumstances need to be taken  seriously and need to be investigated in  a serious  robust independent way robin the trudeau  government is under  increasing pressure to act to address  the allegations  of widespread sexual misconduct in the  military given their self-described  feminist status as a government  okay thanks mercedes and you can watch  more of the interview tomorrow  right here on global on the west block  still ahead no morning just celebrating  meet the man trying to find the bright  side  of death  death is never easy to talk about and  right now it's especially difficult  because we can't gather with friends and  family for comfort that's why a video  posted online by a scottish man about  confronting what he calls  the final taboo caught donna friesen's  eye  and she's decided to take a closer look  for this week's edition  of the new reality donna  robin tom morton is what's called a  funeral celebrant and he's  written a book about it essentially what  he does is help mourners confront the  loss of loved ones  in a positive way to celebrate their  life rather than  try to avoid the topic of death it's  part of the growing  death positive movement and being death  positive himself  morton recently did something kind of  unusual  this is one way to confront the final  taboo  choose your own grave gravesite tom  morton did it  on his 65th birthday this is the plot  which myself and my wife have chosen to  be buried in  unusual intrepid and an odd birthday  gift to yourself  yet intriguing there is that sense of  taboo that you know you mustn't talk  about death or  if you go to a graveyard it will be  terribly spooky and  it will be horrible and these are not  things we want to talk about but  actually i think it's really healthy to  talk about and anticipate these things  funeral celebrants like morton provide  personalized  non-religious services without all the  trappings  of the funeral industry and that's  important especially during the pandemic  at a time when so many families have  delayed funeral services  there is a danger that we're storing up  grief which could be damaging ultimately  to everyone's mental health  it's not all doom and gloom though  morton says undertakers have the best  jokes so we'll have a little bit of that  famous scottish humor too  robin all right thanks donna you can  watch the full story on the new reality  tonight  on global tv up next  size does matter when it comes to the  survival of this species  cell phone video captured this dramatic  encounter at the san diego zoo  it shows an elephant charging a man who  trips and drops the child in his arms  while trying to escape  the man allegedly carried his  two-year-old daughter into the enclosure  to take  photos the 25-year-old father was  arrested on suspicion of  child endangerment when it comes to  brains the sperm whale  has the biggest on earth six times the  size of a human a new study led by  canadian scientists shows how these  animals  used their big brains to avoid being  hunted centuries ago  as ross lord explains researchers found  a pattern that's raising hope  for another endangered whale new  research sheds light on why the sperm  whale which is an endangered species  was not killed off entirely  in the 18th and 19th centuries whaling  ships like the one depicted in the  classic novel and film moby dick  decimated the whale's population but an  international research team studied  newly digitized whaling log books from  the north pacific ocean  and concluded sperm whales made smart  use of their remarkable asset  the biggest brain on earth we have a  real habit of underestimating what other  animals can do  traditionally sperm whale's only  predator was the orca  staying safe meant forming defensive  circles and smashing their tails  outwards  that tactic left them wide open to be  slaughtered when whalers began hunting  them  the new findings bolster a theory that  the whales adjusted  leaving their formations and swimming  upwind to escape from the whalers  who were also powered by the wind so the  whalers  saw a sperm whale and they went after it  but their success in killing it  went down by about 60 percent in three  to five years  whitehead says the whales can  communicate over great distances using  sonar clicks  with family groups using different  dialects he says is possible they warned  each other in the same way they share  information about where to find food and  they use this information  to at least some of the time and and  more and more often as time went on  behave in a way to evade the whalers  finance could raise hope for the  endangered north atlantic right whale  their numbers are dwindling because of  ship strikes and fishing gear  entanglement  in atlanta canada that does give us a  bit of hope that they can  learn effective methods to avoid  the noise the fishing gear the  plastic whitehead suggests creatures  that survived for millions of years  before humans existed  should not be underestimated ross lord  global news and that is global national  for this saturday night i'm robin gill  tonight your canada is petawawa ontario  please keep sending your photos to the  email address you see and i'll see you  back here tomorrow have a great night  you
 

